      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate fragment carefully.\n\nThis is an NMi certificate for a KROHNE Altometer WATERFLUX 3070 electromagnetic water meter.\n\nKey details:\n- Certificate number: R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02, revision 2\n- Project number: 3646819\n- Page total: 5\n- Member state: Netherlands\n- Date issued: 12 June 2023\n- Issuing authority: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Applicant and Manufacturer: KROHNE Altometer, Kerkeplaat 12, 3313 LC Dordrecht, The Netherlands\n- Certified type: water meter (electromagnetic), Type: WATERFLUX 3070\n- Recommendation: R 49-1 (2013) — canonical id \"R49\", edition... hmm, \"R 49-1 (2013)\" — the recommendation is R49, part 1, edition 2013. I'll use id \"R49-1\" or \"R49\"? The schema says canonical e.g. \"R60\" not \"OIML R 60\". The recommendation is \"R 49-1\" — I could use \"R49-1\" as id with edition 2013. Let me use \"R49-1\" since the certificate explicitly references R 49-1. Actually, the canonical form might be \"R49\" but the part is \"-1\". The document says \"R 49-1 (2013)\". I'll use id \"R49-1\", edition... hmm, edition is int. 2013. But actually \"R 49-1 (2013)\" is the 2012 edition... no, it's published 2013? Actually OIML R49-1 was published 2012 (Edition 2012, with amendment?). The certificate says (2013). I should follow the document: edition 2013? Wait, R49-1:2012 exists. Hmm, but the certificate explicitly writes \"R 49-1 (2013)\". I'll record edition as 2013 per the document. Actually the schema says edition (int). The safest is to use what's in the document: \"R 49-1 (2013)\" → id \"R49-1\", edition 2013. Hmm, but that could be wrong if the actual edition is 2012. Don't fabricate — the document says 2013, so edition: 2013.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. OIML R 49-1 was published in 2012 (Edition 2012). But there could be... The certificate says \"R 49-1 (2013)\". Some certificates reference \"R 49-1 (2013)\" which might be the edition year used by NMi. I'll go with 2013 as printed.\n\n- Accuracy class: 1 and 2 → [1, 2]\n\nTest reports:\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-09.01, dated 16 September 2009, 41 pages, 14 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-10.01, dated 9 April 2010, 40 pages, 3 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-11.01, dated 2 May 2011, 40 pages, 4 annexes\n- R49-1/2006-NL1-12.01, dated 28 March 2012, 40 pages, 3 annexes\n- NMi-13200194-01, dated 18 July 2013, 2 pages, 1 annex\n- NMi-13200159-01, dated 18 March 2015, 6 pages, 1 annex\n- NMi-15200645-01, dated 30 March 2016, 21 pages, 4 annexes\n- NMi-2224507-01, dated 30 December 2020, 27 pages, 4 annexes\n\nThese are \"associated reports\" — role: type_evaluation_report probably. The first mentions \"OIML Type Evaluation Report\". I'll use role \"type_evaluation_report\" for all, or \"test_report\"? They're described as \"tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". The schema allows: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. I'll use \"type_evaluation_report\" for all since they're the OIML Type Evaluation Report series. Hmm, some might be test reports. Given ambiguity, \"type_evaluation_report\" seems reasonable. Actually the certificate says \"the associated reports\" and the certificate references \"OIML Type Evaluation Report\". I'll mark all as \"type_evaluation_report\".\n\nAlso \"Documentation folder no. T10201-11\" — that's a documentation_file. Include in test_reports with role \"documentation_file\"? The schema says test_reports[]: id, date, pages, role. The documentation folder has no date. I could include it with id T10201-11, role documentation_file. That seems reasonable since rule 8 says don't fabricate — date is not present so omit. But can I omit fields within the object? Yes, \"omit sections that are not present\" — for missing fields, omit or null. I'll include it.\n\nTable 1 General characteristics:\n- Measuring principle: Electromagnetic → type_level characteristic: measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- Accuracy class: 1 and 2 (already in recommendation.accuracy_classes)\n- Environmental class: M2 or O (outdoors, sw ≥ 5.0.1_) or B (in building, sw ≤ 4.3.1_). This is config-dependent (software version). Hmm. Environmental class varies by installation/software. This is tricky. I could record as type_level with value string, or config_level. The value: \"M2\" generally? Actually it says \"M2<br>O (installed outdoors) for software version 5.0.1_ or higher<br>B (installed in a building) for software version 4.3.1_ or lower\". So environmental class M2 overall (R49 environmental classes are A, B, C plus O, M1, M2, M3). Actually in R49-1:2012, environmental classes: A, B, C; and special classes O (outdoor), M1, M2, M3. So \"M2\" is the class, with O and B sub-details. Hmm, actually the text: \"M2 / O (installed outdoors) for sw 5.0.1+ / B (installed in building) for sw 4.3.1 or lower\". \n\nThis is a conditional table. I could model as config_level with axis \"software_version_and_installation\". Or simpler: type_level environmental_class with value \"M2\" — but that loses info. Let me use config_level:\n\n```\n{\"attribute\": \"environmental_class\", \"axis\": \"software_version_and_installation\", \"values\": [\n  {\"condition\": \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher, installed outdoors\", \"value\": \"O\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"software version 4.3.1_ or lower, installed in a building\", \"value\": \"B\"}\n]}\n```\n\nHmm, but \"M2\" is listed first. In R49-1, classes are M1, M2, M3 for mechanical environment and O for outdoor. Actually R49-1:2012 defines: environmental classes A, B, C (climatic), O (outdoor installations), and mechanical classes M1, M2, M3. The table entry \"M2 / O (installed outdoors)...\" — maybe means M2 or O depending... The value is \"M2\" with conditional detail. Actually I think the intent: environmental class is M2; for outdoor installations with sw ≥ 5.0.1 it's O; for indoor with sw ≤ 4.3.1 it's B. \n\nTo be safe, I'll do config_level with conditions:\n- {\"condition\": \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher, installed outdoors\", \"value\": \"O\"}\n- {\"condition\": \"software version 4.3.1_ or lower, installed in a building\", \"value\": \"B\"}\n- {\"condition\": \"base class\", \"value\": \"M2\"}? That's weird.\n\nAlternative: type_level environmental_class value \"M2\" and separate config entries. Hmm. The markdown literally: \"M2<br>O (installed outdoors) for software version 5.0.1_ or higher<br>B (installed in a building) for software version 4.3.1_ or lower\". The first cell \"M2\" might be a stray from rowspan or just class M2. I think it means: Environmental class M2; OR O... no wait. Actually in NMi certificates for WATERFLUX 3070, environmental class is \"M2\" for the mechanical/vibration class and climatic/environment \"O\" outdoor or \"B\" indoor. Hmm.\n\nLet me just capture config_level:\n```\n{\"attribute\": \"environmental_class\", \"axis\": \"software_version_and_installation\", \"values\": [\n  {\"condition\": \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher, installed outdoors\", \"value\": \"O\", \"unit_symbol\": null},\n  {\"condition\": \"software version 4.3.1_ or lower, installed in a building\", \"value\": \"B\", \"unit_symbol\": null}\n]}\n```\nand maybe include M2 as the general class: value at type_level: environmental_class \"M2\"? That could be contradictory. Alternatively include a third condition {\"condition\": null, \"value\": \"M2\"}. Hmm, the schema config_level values: {condition, value}. I'll include three entries: M2 general? That misrepresents.\n\nActually re-reading: \"Environmental class: M2<br>O (installed outdoors) for software version 5.0.1_ or higher<br>B (installed in a building) for software version 4.3.1_ or lower\". I think the first is \"M2\" alone — maybe M2 applies generally? But then O and B are climatic/environment classes conditioned on software version. Given ambiguity, I'll record environmental_class_mechanical? No, don't invent.\n\nI'll do: type_level: environmental_class: {value: \"M2\"} and config_level entries for the conditional O/B with axis \"software_version\". Wait but that duplicates/conflicts. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively treat the whole as config_level:\n- condition: null/\"\" → value \"M2\"? \n\nLet me look at actual NMi cert for WATERFLUX 3070 (R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02 rev 2). From memory of such certs: \"Environmental class: M2 / O (installed outdoors) for software version 5.0.1_ or higher / B (installed in a building) for software version 4.3.1_ or lower\". I believe in R49, environmental classes include M1, M2, M3 (mechanical environment: influence quantities like vibration) and O (outdoor). Actually R49-1:2012 Table: Environmental classes: A, B, C with O for outdoor; mechanical environment M1, M2, M3; electromagnetic E1, E2. The certificate lists \"Environmental class\" with M2 — hmm.\n\nGiven ambiguity, I'll go config_level with three conditions where first has condition null meaning general M2? The schema for config_level values: {condition, value}. I'll write:\n\n```\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"environmental_class\",\n  \"axis\": \"software_version_and_installation\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": null, \"value\": \"M2\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"installed outdoors, software version 5.0.1_ or higher\", \"value\": \"O\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"installed in a building, software version 4.3.1_ or lower\", \"value\": \"B\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nHmm, condition null is odd. Maybe better: keep M2 as type_level since it appears unconditional-ish. Actually, I think M2 might be the overall environmental class per R49 and the O/B lines describe the class depending on install location & sw version. Honestly, the \"M2\" first cell probably means class M2 for... ugh.\n\nDecision: I'll put it entirely in config_level with the first entry condition \"general\" → \"M2\". Actually no — simpler and defensible: \n\nvalues: [\n  {\"condition\": \"general\", \"value\": \"M2\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"installed outdoors, software version 5.0.1_ or higher\", \"value\": \"O\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"installed in a building, software version 4.3.1_ or lower\", \"value\": \"B\"}\n]\n\nHmm, \"condition\": \"general\" is fabrication-ish. Let me use null for condition on first. The schema example shows condition present; null should be acceptable.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider: maybe the environmental class is \"M2 or O or B\" — i.e., the meter meets class M2; additionally class O for outdoor (sw≥5.0.1) and class B indoor (sw≤4.3.1). In R49-1:2012, \"environmental class\" (clause 4.2) includes A, B, C, O. M1-M3 are \"mechanical environment\" classes. So \"M2\" here is likely the mechanical class listed under environmental. So:\n- type_level: mechanical_environment_class? No — label as given: \"Environmental class: M2\". I'll keep environmental_class at config level for O/B conditioned on sw version & installation, and type_level environmental_class value \"M2\".\n\nHmm, conflicts. You know, I'll just do config_level with all three, conditions:\n1. {\"condition\": null, \"value\": \"M2\"}\n2. {\"condition\": \"installed outdoors, software version 5.0.1_ or higher\", \"value\": \"O\"}\n3. {\"condition\": \"installed in a building, software version 4.3.1_ or lower\", \"value\": \"B\"}\n\nThat preserves all info without over-interpreting. Good enough.\n\n- Electromagnetic environment: E2 → type_level: electromagnetic_environment_class? label \"electromagnetic_environment\": value \"E2\".\n\n- Temperature range ambient: -25°C/+55°C for sw 5.0.1_+, -10°C/+55°C for sw 4.3.1_- → config_level:\n```\n{\"attribute\": \"ambient_temperature_range\", \"axis\": \"software_version\", \"values\": [\n  {\"condition\": \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher\", \"value\": {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"software version 4.3.1_ or lower\", \"value\": {\"min\": -10, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n]}\n```\nNote schema shows config_level values {condition, value} — I'll add unit_symbol and unit_id to be consistent with structured values rule.\n\n- Water temperature class: T50 (+0,1 °C / +50 °C) → type_level: water_temperature_class: value \"T50\"; plus water_temperature_range {min: 0.1, max: 50} °C? The T50 class in R49 is 0.1–50 °C. I'll record:\n  - water_temperature_class: {value: \"T50\"}\n  - maybe water_temperature_range: {value: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}. The \"+0,1\" is 0.1. I'll include both; it's faithful.\n\n- Maximum admissible pressure (MAP): 1,6 MPa (16 bar) → maximum_admissible_pressure: {value: 1.6, unit_symbol: \"MPa\"}; note also 16 bar. Maybe add secondary? I'll record value 1.6 MPa and include a note? Schema doesn't have notes for type_level. I could add two: maximum_admissible_pressure (MPa, 1.6) — the (16 bar) parenthetical is equivalent. I'll just do one with value 1.6 MPa. Or two entries. Keep one; maybe include \"maximum_admissible_pressure\" with value 1.6 and also map_equivalent? Skip equivalence to avoid noise. Actually including both is fine: I'll include just the primary 1.6 MPa.\n\n- Orientation: All positions (Horizontal, vertical or diagonal) → orientation: {value: \"All positions\"}? or \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\". Keep string \"All positions\".\n\n- Flow profile sensitivity class: U0 and D0 (0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream) → flow_profile_sensitivity_class: {value: \"U0 and D0\"}; maybe flow_profile_installation: \"0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream\" as separate characteristic: straight_pipe_length_requirement? I'll record flow_profile_sensitivity_class \"U0 and D0\" and note in value string the 0 x DN? Better: two type_level chars:\n  - flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0\"\n  - straight_pipe_requirements: \"0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream\" — label maybe \"flow_conditioning\": hmm. I'll use \"straight_pipe_length_upstream_dn\": 0 and downstream 0? That's nice: upstream_dn: 0, downstream_dn: 0. But labels should reflect content: \"upstream_straight_pipe_length\" value 0 unit \"DN\". Eh. I'll do:\n  - flow_profile_sensitivity_class: {value: \"U0 and D0\"}\n  - straight_pipe_length_requirement: {value: \"0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream\"}? A bit verbose but faithful. Alternatively omit. I'll include the sensitivity class only, and put \"0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream\" as separate char \"flow_conditioning_requirement\". Hmm, maybe simpler: include in sensitivity class string? Rule: keep faithful. I'll add separate: \"flow_profile_installation\": {value: \"0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream\"}.\n\n- Reverse flow: The sensor is designed to measure reverse flow → reverse_flow: {value: \"designed to measure reverse flow\"} or boolean-ish string. I'll use value: \"The sensor is designed to measure reverse flow\". Shorter: \"reverse flow measured\". Keep as string: \"designed to measure reverse flow\".\n\n- Pressure loss class: Δp 63 (0,63 bar) → pressure_loss_class: {value: \"Δp 63\"}; pressure_loss: {value: 0.63, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}? The class Δp63 corresponds to 0.63 bar. I'll add both:\n  - pressure_loss_class: \"Δp63\" (canonical, no spaces) — value \"Δp 63\" fine.\n  - maximum_pressure_loss: {value: 0.63, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}\n  \n  Hmm Δp symbol — should avoid weird chars? Δ is fine Unicode.\n\n- Power supply: multi-part:\n  \"3,6 Volts D-cell Lithium replaceable battery; or\n  Flexpower for software version 5.0.1_ or higher:\n  - 3,6 Volts D-cell Lithium battery (non-replaceable battery)\n  - 9 … 30 VDC\n  - 100...230 VAC / 50-60Hz (+/-2 %)\"\n\nThis is a config-level (power source options) situation. Options:\ntype_level:\n- battery_voltage: {value: 3.6, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- battery_type: \"D-cell Lithium replaceable\"\nconfig_level for alternatives:\n```\n{\"attribute\": \"power_supply\", \"axis\": \"power_supply_option\", \"values\": [\n  {\"condition\": \"battery powered\", \"value\": \"3.6 V D-cell lithium replaceable battery\", \"unit_symbol\": null},\n  {\"condition\": \"Flexpower, software version 5.0.1_ or higher, battery\", \"value\": \"3.6 V D-cell lithium battery (non-replaceable)\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"Flexpower, software version 5.0.1_ or higher, DC supply\", \"value\": \"9...30 V DC\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"Flexpower, software version 5.0.1_ or higher, AC supply\", \"value\": \"100...230 V AC / 50-60 Hz (±2%)\"}\n]}\n```\n\nStructured rule says pull units out. For config values, structured form {condition, value, unit_symbol}. For DC: value {min 9, max 30}, unit_symbol \"V DC\"? unit \"V\". type DC. Let me produce separate config entries:\n\n- power_supply_voltage, axis \"power_supply_option\":\n  - condition \"replaceable D-cell lithium battery\": value 3.6, unit \"V\"\n  - condition \"non-replaceable D-cell lithium battery (Flexpower, sw 5.0.1_+)\": value 3.6, unit \"V\"\n  - condition \"DC supply (Flexpower, sw 5.0.1_+)\": value {min 9, max 30}, unit \"V\" (DC)\n  - condition \"AC supply (Flexpower, sw 5.0.1_+)\": value {min 100, max 230}, unit \"V\" (AC)\n- power_supply_frequency: condition AC: {min 50, max 60}, \"Hz\", tolerance ±2% — tolerance maybe footnote-ish; add to condition string \"(±2 %)\".\n\nThat's elaborate but per rules (split voltage/frequency/type). Let me structure:\n\nconfig_level entries:\n1. {\"attribute\": \"power_supply_voltage\", \"axis\": \"power_supply_option\", \"values\": [\n   {\"condition\": \"replaceable 3.6 V D-cell lithium battery\", \"value\": 3.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"non-replaceable D-cell lithium battery (Flexpower, software version 5.0.1_ or higher)\", \"value\": 3.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"DC power supply (Flexpower, software version 5.0.1_ or higher)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 9, \"max\": 30}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"AC power supply (Flexpower, software version 5.0.1_ or higher)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 100, \"max\": 230}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"}\n]}\n2. {\"attribute\": \"power_supply_frequency\", \"axis\": \"power_supply_option\", \"values\": [\n   {\"condition\": \"AC power supply (Flexpower, software version 5.0.1_ or higher, ±2 %)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\"}\n]}\n3. {\"attribute\": \"power_supply_type\", \"axis\": \"power_supply_option\", \"values\": [\n   {\"condition\": \"battery\", \"value\": \"DC (battery)\"},\n   ...]} — maybe too noisy. I'll include power_supply_type with values: battery / DC / AC.\n   {\"condition\": \"DC power supply (Flexpower, software version 5.0.1_ or higher)\", \"value\": \"DC\"},\n   {\"condition\": \"AC power supply (Flexpower, software version 5.0.1_ or higher)\", \"value\": \"AC\"}\n   ]}\n\nAnd battery description char: battery_type: \"D-cell lithium\" maybe. Also \"replaceable\" vs \"non-replaceable\" captured in conditions. OK.\n\n- Software identification table (page 3): software versions with CRC checksums:\n  - 4.0.4_, 4.0.10_, 4.0.11_, 4.0.12_, 4.2.2_, 4.2.4_, 4.2.5_, 4.2.6_, 4.3.0_, 4.3.1_ → Not applicable\n  - 5.0.1_ → 4Cb5\n  - 5.0.2_ → 71d5\n  - 5.0.3_ → CFF7\n  - 5.0.5_ → dCAb\n  - 5.1.0_ → Ab62\n\nThis could be matrix_tables with columns software_version, crc_checksum. Good candidate for matrix_tables:\n\n```\n{\n  \"name\": \"Software identification\",\n  \"columns\": [\"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"software_version\": \"4.0.4_\", \"crc_checksum\": \"Not applicable\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"4.0.10_\", \"crc_checksum\": \"Not applicable\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"4.0.11_\", \"crc_checksum\": \"Not applicable\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"4.0.12_\", \"crc_checksum\": \"Not applicable\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"4.2.2_\", \"crc_checksum\": \"Not applicable\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"4.2.4_\", \"crc_checksum\": \"Not applicable\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"4.2.5_\", \"crc_checksum\": \"Not applicable\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"4.2.6_\", \"crc_checksum\": \"Not applicable\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"4.3.0_\", \"crc_checksum\": \"Not applicable\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"4.3.1_\", \"crc_checksum\": \"Not applicable\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"5.0.1_\", \"crc_checksum\": \"4Cb5\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"5.0.2_\", \"crc_checksum\": \"71d5\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"5.0.3_\", \"crc_checksum\": \"CFF7\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"5.0.5_\", \"crc_checksum\": \"dCAb\"},\n    {\"software_version\": \"5.1.0_\", \"crc_checksum\": \"Ab62\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nWait — the first row groups 10 versions in one cell. Should I split into individual rows? The table has one row with 10 versions and \"Not applicable\". Splitting into separate rows is faithful and more machine-readable. I'll split them.\n\nNote: \"In case of software version 4.3.1_ or lower the Field Current can only be set to 16 mA.\" → type_level: field_current: {value: 16, unit_symbol: \"mA\"} with condition? It's conditional. config_level:\n{\"attribute\": \"field_current\", \"axis\": \"software_version\", \"values\": [\n  {\"condition\": \"software version 4.3.1_ or lower\", \"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"mA\"},\n  {\"condition\": \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher\", \"value\": \"selectable, shall be set to 16 mA\", \"unit_symbol\": \"mA\"}\n]}\nThe second: \"the different Field Currents can be selected and shall be set to 16 mA\". Hmm — value 16 mA applies but selection allowed. I'll encode as string for second condition: \"selectable; shall be set to 16 mA\". Keep unit_symbol null for the string. Or value: 16 with condition \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher (different field currents selectable)\". I'll do value 16 mA for both, with conditions noting the difference:\n- {\"condition\": \"software version 4.3.1_ or lower (only setting)\", \"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"mA\"}\n- {\"condition\": \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher (multiple selectable; shall be set to 16 mA)\", \"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"mA\"}\n\nOK.\n\nTable 2 (pages 3–4): family of instruments with meter sizes DN25–DN600, accuracy class, Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, ratio Q3/Q1.\n\nThe table has complex rowspan: each DN can have multiple rows (different class/Q3/Q1 combos). Let me parse each:\n\nDN25: class 2 (rowspan 2), two rows:\n  - Q1 0.025, Q2 0.04, Q3 10, Q4 12.5, ratio 400\n  - Q1 0.04, Q2 0.064, Q3 16, Q4 20, ratio 400\nDN40: class 2:\n  - 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.25, 400\n  - 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\nDN50: class 2:\n  - 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400\n  - 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\nDN65: class 2 (2 rows): \n  - 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400\n  - 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n  class 1 (1 row): 0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, 250\nDN80: class 2 (2 rows):\n  - 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400\n  - 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n  class 1 (2 rows):\n  - 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160\n  - 0.64, 1.024, 160, 200, 250\nDN100: class 2 (2 rows):\n  - 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400\n  - 0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400\n  class 1 (2 rows):\n  - 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160\n  - 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250\nDN125: class 2 (2 rows):\n  - 0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400\n  - 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\n  class 1 (2 rows):\n  - 1.5625, 2.5, 250, 312.5, 160\n  - 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250\nDN150: class 2 (2 rows):\n  - 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400\n  - 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\n  class 1 (2 rows):\n  - 2.5, 4, 400, 500, 160\n  - 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250\nDN200: class 2: 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400\n  class 1: 3.9375, 6.3, 630, 787.5, 160\nDN250: class 2: 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400\n  class 1: 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160\nDN300: class 2: 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400\n  class 1: 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160\nDN350: class \"1 or 2\": 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160\nDN400: class \"1 or 2\": 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160\nDN450: class \"1 or 2\": 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160\nDN500: class \"1 or 2\": 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160\nDN600: class \"1 or 2\": 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100\n\nTotal rows: DN25:2, DN40:2, DN50:2, DN65:3, DN80:4, DN100:4, DN125:4, DN150:4, DN200:2, DN250:2, DN300:2, DN350:1, DN400:1, DN450:1, DN500:1, DN600:1 = 36 rows.\n\nDecimal conversion: 0,025 → 0.025; 12,5 → 12.5; 312,5 → 312.5; 78,75 → 78.75; 100,8 → 100.8.\n\nModel family: models — variants are DN sizes (with per-class/Q3 combos). The model_id could be \"DN25\", \"DN40\", etc. But there are multiple variants per DN (different Q3 and ratio). The R137 example uses model_id per size. Here per DN there can be up to 4 combos. I think best: models per row-combination: e.g. \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\" or just list DN sizes as models with attributes in model_level values referencing... The model_level values need {model, value}. If multiple rows per DN, ambiguous.\n\nApproach: use matrix_tables for the full table (36 rows), each row with meter_size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1. And populate model_family.models with one entry per unique combination, model_id like \"DN25\" — hmm but then model_level can't distinguish.\n\nGiven the complexity, I think the cleanest: model_family.models with model_id per DN size (16 models: DN25...DN600), and model_level characteristics q1/q2/q3/q4 would have multiple values per model — not allowed cleanly.\n\nAlternative: model_id per table row: \"DN25/Q3=10\", \"DN25/Q3=16\", etc. That's more precise. The model is really distinguished by DN + Q3 (nominal flow) + ratio. E.g., \"DN25, Q3=10 m³/h\". I'll use model_id like \"DN25-Q3=10\" and label maybe. 36 models. That's a lot but accurate.\n\nHmm, is that overkill? The schema example R137 had one row per model. Here multiple rows per DN. I think per-row models is the faithful representation. Let me generate model_id as \"DN25|class 2|Q3=10\" — verbose. Simpler: \"DN25 (Q3 10 m³/h)\". Let me think about what's most useful and consistent: model_id: \"DN25-Q3=10\", label: \"DN25, accuracy class 2, Q3 10 m³/h, Q3/Q1 400\"? Maybe too much. \n\nI'll do model_id like \"DN25-Q3=10\" and attributes minimal; matrix_tables holds the full data. And model_level characteristics: q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, accuracy_class per model.\n\nActually, should accuracy_class be in model_level? It varies per row (class 1 vs 2 vs \"1 or 2\"). Yes, include as model_level attribute with numeric/string values: 1, 2, or \"1 or 2\". Hmm, \"1 or 2\" for DN350-600 means both classes available at same flows. Keep as string \"1 or 2\".\n\nLet me now assemble model list with ids. I'll define model_id per row:\n\n1. DN25, class 2, Q3=10 → \"DN25-Q3=10\"\n2. DN25, class 2, Q3=16 → \"DN25-Q3=16\"\n3. DN40, class 2, Q3=25 → \"DN40-Q3=25\"\n4. DN40, class 2, Q3=40 → \"DN40-Q3=40\"\n5. DN50, class 2, Q3=40 → \"DN50-Q3=40\"\n6. DN50, class 2, Q3=63 → \"DN50-Q3=63\"\n7. DN65, class 2, Q3=63 → \"DN65-Q3=63\"\n8. DN65, class 2, Q3=100 → \"DN65-Q3=100\"\n9. DN65, class 1, Q3=100 → \"DN65-class1-Q3=100\" — conflict with row 8 same DN & Q3 but different class. So id must include class or ratio. Row 8 ratio 400, row 9 ratio 250. Use \"DN65-Q3=100-ratio=400\" etc.? Getting messy.\n\nAlternative simpler: sequential model ids based on DN and Q3 and ratio: \"DN65-Q3=100-Q1=0.25\"? Ugh.\n\nMaybe better: matrix_tables carries the full fidelity; model_family.models can list unique meter sizes DN25...DN600 (16 models), and model_level characteristics only include those that map 1:1 per DN — none of the flows map 1:1 (except DN350-600 where single row each). Hmm.\n\nPragmatic decision: I'll create models per table row with model_id encoding DN, class, and Q3: e.g. \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\", \"DN65 class 1 Q3=100\". This is unambiguous and readable. 36 models.\n\nLet me enumerate all rows carefully:\n\nDN25:\n r1: class 2, Q1 0.025, Q2 0.04, Q3 10, Q4 12.5, ratio 400 → id \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\"\n r2: class 2, Q1 0.04, Q2 0.064, Q3 16, Q4 20, ratio 400 → \"DN25 class 2 Q3=16\"\nDN40:\n r3: class 2, 0.0625, 0.1, 25, 31.25, 400 → \"DN40 class 2 Q3=25\"\n r4: class 2, 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400 → \"DN40 class 2 Q3=40\"\nDN50:\n r5: class 2, 0.1, 0.16, 40, 50, 400 → \"DN50 class 2 Q3=40\"\n r6: class 2, 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400 → \"DN50 class 2 Q3=63\"\nDN65:\n r7: class 2, 0.1575, 0.252, 63, 78.75, 400 → \"DN65 class 2 Q3=63\"\n r8: class 2, 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400 → \"DN65 class 2 Q3=100\"\n r9: class 1, 0.4, 0.64, 100, 125, 250 → \"DN65 class 1 Q3=100\"\nDN80:\n r10: class 2, 0.25, 0.4, 100, 125, 400 → \"DN80 class 2 Q3=100\"\n r11: class 2, 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400 → \"DN80 class 2 Q3=160\"\n r12: class 1, 0.625, 1, 100, 125, 160 → \"DN80 class 1 Q3=100\"\n r13: class 1, 0.64, 1.024, 160, 200, 250 → \"DN80 class 1 Q3=160\"\nDN100:\n r14: class 2, 0.4, 0.64, 160, 200, 400 → \"DN100 class 2 Q3=160\"\n r15: class 2, 0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400 → \"DN100 class 2 Q3=250\"\n r16: class 1, 1, 1.6, 160, 200, 160 → \"DN100 class 1 Q3=160\"\n r17: class 1, 1, 1.6, 250, 312.5, 250 → \"DN100 class 1 Q3=250\"\nDN125:\n r18: class 2, 0.625, 1, 250, 312.5, 400 → \"DN125 class 2 Q3=250\"\n r19: class 2, 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400 → \"DN125 class 2 Q3=400\"\n r20: class 1, 1.5625, 2.5, 250, 312.5, 160 → \"DN125 class 1 Q3=250\"\n r21: class 1, 1.6, 2.56, 400, 500, 250 → \"DN125 class 1 Q3=400\"\nDN150:\n r22: class 2, 1, 1.6, 400, 500, 400 → \"DN150 class 2 Q3=400\"\n r23: class 2, 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400 → \"DN150 class 2 Q3=630\"\n r24: class 1, 2.5, 4, 400, 500, 160 → \"DN150 class 1 Q3=400\"\n r25: class 1, 2.52, 4.032, 630, 787.5, 250 → \"DN150 class 1 Q3=630\"\nDN200:\n r26: class 2, 1.575, 2.52, 630, 787.5, 400 → \"DN200 class 2 Q3=630\"\n r27: class 1, 3.9375, 6.3, 630, 787.5, 160 → \"DN200 class 1 Q3=630\"\nDN250:\n r28: class 2, 2.5, 4, 1000, 1250, 400 → \"DN250 class 2 Q3=1000\"\n r29: class 1, 6.25, 10, 1000, 1250, 160 → \"DN250 class 1 Q3=1000\"\nDN300:\n r30: class 2, 4, 6.4, 1600, 2000, 400 → \"DN300 class 2 Q3=1600\"\n r31: class 1, 10, 16, 1600, 2000, 160 → \"DN300 class 1 Q3=1600\"\nDN350:\n r32: class \"1 or 2\", 15.625, 25, 2500, 3125, 160 → \"DN350 class 1 or 2 Q3=2500\"\nDN400:\n r33: class \"1 or 2\", 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160 → \"DN400 class 1 or 2 Q3=4000\"\nDN450:\n r34: class \"1 or 2\", 25, 40, 4000, 5000, 160 → \"DN450 class 1 or 2 Q3=4000\"\nDN500:\n r35: class \"1 or 2\", 39.375, 63, 6300, 7875, 160 → \"DN500 class 1 or 2 Q3=6300\"\nDN600:\n r36: class \"1 or 2\", 63, 100.8, 6300, 7875, 100 → \"DN600 class 1 or 2 Q3=6300\"\n\n36 models.\n\nmatrix_tables entry:\n{\n  \"name\": \"General characteristics of the family of instruments (flow rates)\",\n  \"columns\": [\"meter_size\", \"accuracy_class\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"ratio_q3_q1\"],\n  \"column_units\": [null, null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null],\n  \"rows\": [36 objects],\n  \"description\": \"Table 2 General characteristics of the family of instruments; flow rates Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4 in m³/h.\"\n}\n\nmodel_level characteristics:\n- q1, q2, q3, q4 (unit m³/h), ratio_q3_q1 (dimensionless), accuracy_class.\n\nEach with 36 values referencing model ids. That's heavy but per instructions (\"populate model_family.models and characteristics.model_level\"). I'll do it.\n\nFor accuracy_class values: 2, 1, or \"1 or 2\". Values in model_level entries: {\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": ...}. Fine.\n\nFor \"1 or 2\" — string value. OK.\n\ntype_designations: Type: WATERFLUX 3070. type_designations: [\"WATERFLUX 3070\"]. category: \"Water meter\". The category list in schema examples: \"Water meter\" — yes use \"Water meter\".\n\nmodule_designation: not applicable/not mentioned → omit or null. The certified type is a complete water meter (self-contained). I'll omit.\n\nmodel_family.family_name: null (no explicit family name; Table 2 says \"family of instruments\"). Could set family_name null.\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02\"\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- revision: \"2\" — schema doesn't list revision in certificate but revision_history exists. The certificate number includes \"revision 2\". There's no revision history in fragment. Hmm — number field: should I include revision in number? The number as printed: \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02 revision 2\". I'll put number \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02\" and... there's no certificate.revision field in schema. Maybe add \"revision\": \"2\"? Rule 8: don't fabricate fields not in schema. But adding fields isn't forbidden explicitly; safer to keep schema. I could encode number as \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02\" and add revision_history? No changes info. Hmm. The instruction says revision_history[] with revision string. Not present (no history table). I'll add \"revision\": \"2\" to certificate — it's informative and matches document. Actually many extractors include it. The schema yaml lists specific fields but doesn't say \"no additional properties\". I'll include revision: \"2\" as it's genuinely present in the document. Hmm, risk: strict schema validation. The prompt says \"produce a JSON object following the layered schema\". Adding a small field like revision seems acceptable and useful. I'll include it.\n\n- project_number: \"3646819\"\n- page_total: 5 (from \"Page 1 of 5\")\n- member_state: \"The Netherlands\"\n- date_issued: \"2023-06-12\" (12 June 2023). Format: ISO or as printed? Use \"2023-06-12\". The instruction says date fields; I'll use ISO.\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\" (from \"OIML Issuing Authority NL1\"). Yes.\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\" (printed \"— M.Ph.D. Schmidt\"). I'll use \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\".\n- person_title: \"Certification Board\"? The markdown shows \"Certification Board\" near signature — plausibly the title. It says \"Issuing Authority — NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1 / 12 June 2023 / Certification Board\". The title \"Certification Board\" seems to be the signer's role. I'll set person_title: \"Certification Board\". Hmm, could be the board name, not person title. It appears after the date, before NMi address. I'll include person_title \"Certification Board\" — reasonable.\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\" — also \"NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\". Include.\n\napplicants: [{name: \"KROHNE Altometer\", address_lines: [\"Kerkeplaat 12\", \"3313 LC Dordrecht\", \"The Netherlands\"]}]\n\nmanufacturers: same — \"Applicant and Manufacturer — KROHNE Altometer\". Include same entry.\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49-1\"? The document: \"R 49-1 (2013)\". Canonical: \"R49\" not \"OIML R 60\" example. With part: \"R49-1\". I'll use id \"R49-1\". Hmm, \"R 49-1\" might be the 2013 Edition of R49-1? Actual OIML R49-1 Edition 2012... The certificate says (2013). Follow document: edition: 2013.\nWait, should id be \"R49\" with part noted? I'll use \"R49-1\" — closer to printed.\n- edition: 2013\n- amendment: null/omit — not mentioned. Omit.\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [1, 2]\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"WATERFLUX 3070\"]\n- description: \"An electromagnetic water meter\" — description field free text: \"Electromagnetic water meter\". I'll put \"An electromagnetic water meter\".\n\ntest_reports: 8 entries with role. The first bullet (2009) is the original Type Evaluation Report; later ones are extensions. Role for all: \"type_evaluation_report\"? Or \"test_report\" for later ones? Without more info, use \"type_evaluation_report\" for all — they're described as the associated reports. Hmm, NMi-13200194-01 (2 pages, 1 annex) etc. are likely examination reports adding variants. I'll use \"type_evaluation_report\" uniformly — defensible.\n\nAlso documentation: \"Documentation folder no. T10201-11\" → test_reports entry with id \"T10201-11\", role \"documentation_file\". Pages unknown — omit. Date unknown — omit.\n\nfootnotes: none defined in fragment. The \"Note:\" on page 3 about Field Current — that's a note, not a footnote with marker. I captured it as config_level. Could also skip footnotes array entirely. Omit.\n\ncharacteristics.type_level entries:\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic\"\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E2\" (label electromagnetic_environment)\n- water_temperature_class: \"T50\"\n- water_temperature_range: {min: 0.1, max: 50}, \"°C\" (from \"+0,1 °C / +50 °C\")\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6, \"MPa\" — label maximum_admissible_pressure (MAP). Also 16 bar equivalence — skip or include? I'll include as a second char maximum_admissible_pressure_bar? No — messy. Skip. Actually maybe include in matrix? No. Skip.\n- orientation: \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\"\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0 and D0\"\n- flow_profile_installation: \"0 x DN upstream and 0 x DN downstream\" — or split into two numeric chars. I'll keep as one string char labeled \"straight_pipe_length_requirement\". Hmm label choices: keep \"flow_profile_installation\" closer to source? The source phrase is inside parentheses explaining U0/D0. I'll add \"straight_pipe_length_upstream_dn\": 0 and \"straight_pipe_length_downstream_dn\": 0 — that's structured and clean. unit_symbol \"DN\". Good.\n- reverse_flow: \"designed to measure reverse flow\" — label reverse_flow, value string. Or boolean true with description lost. String is fine.\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp63\"? Original \"Δp 63 (0,63 bar)\". value \"Δp63\", plus maximum_pressure_loss 0.63 bar. I'll record pressure_loss_class: \"Δp63\" and pressure_loss: {value: 0.63, unit_symbol: \"bar\"}.\n- accuracy_class: [1,2]? Already in recommendation. Skip in type_level.\n\nconfig_level entries (axis):\n1. environmental_class (axis \"software_version_and_installation\"): values: M2 (condition null), O (outdoors, sw≥5.0.1_), B (in building, sw≤4.3.1_).\n\nHmm wait — let me reconsider the environmental class semantics once more. R49-1:2012 environmental classes: climatic/environment classes A, B, C, O; mechanical classes M1, M2, M3. The cert lists \"Environmental class: M2 / O (installed outdoors)... / B (installed in building)...\". So M2 is the mechanical class, and O or B the environmental (climatic) class. But the label in table is just \"Environmental class\". To stay faithful, one config_level attribute \"environmental_class\" with the three entries is fine. Or split: mechanical_environment_class \"M2\" at type_level and environmental_class config with O/B. That might actually be the most accurate: \n- type_level: mechanical_environment_class? But the table says \"Environmental class: M2...\" — renaming to mechanical is interpretation. Hmm. But it's a well-known R49 fact. Risk of \"fabrication\"? Mild. I'll keep single attribute environmental_class with three config values, conditions for O and B, first with condition null. Safer.\n\n2. ambient_temperature_range (axis software_version): \n   - condition \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher\": {min: -25, max: 55} °C\n   - condition \"software version 4.3.1_ or lower\": {min: -10, max: 55} °C\n\n3. field_current (axis software_version):\n   - condition \"software version 4.3.1_ or lower\": 16 mA (only setting)\n   - condition \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher\": selectable, shall be set to 16 mA → value 16 mA with condition noting selection. I'll write condition \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher (multiple field currents selectable)\" value 16 mA.\n\n4. power_supply_voltage (axis power_supply_option): 4 values as above.\n5. power_supply_frequency (axis power_supply_option): AC 50–60 Hz (±2%).\n6. power_supply_type (axis power_supply_option): battery / DC / AC.\n\nAlso battery characteristics: battery_voltage 3.6 V — captured in power_supply_voltage conditions. battery type D-cell lithium — in conditions. Good enough.\n\nmodel_level: q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1, accuracy_class across 36 models. Let me also consider meter_size itself as model attribute — it's encoded in model_id; fine.\n\nLet me write values arrays. For each attribute, 36 entries. That's 5-6 × 36 = ~200 value objects. Large but OK. Actually, do I need model_level AND matrix_tables both? The R137 example shows both. I'll include both — instructions explicitly say extract as matrix_tables AND populate model_family + model_level.\n\nHmm, that will make the JSON huge. It's fine.\n\nWait — maybe I can reduce: keep model_level for q1,q2,q3,q4, ratio; accuracy_class values too. Yes.\n\nAlso DN size: I could add model_level attribute \"meter_size_dn\" with value per model (25, 40, ... 600, unit \"DN\" or \"mm\"?). DN25 means nominal diameter 25 (mm). unit_symbol \"mm\"? DN is dimensionless nominal size. I'll add \"nominal_size_dn\": value 25 etc., unit null or \"DN\". I'll include unit_symbol \"DN\". Actually to keep it simpler, skip — model_id encodes it. But R137 example didn't include size as model_level either (size column in matrix + model_id). Follow that: skip nominal_size in model_level; it's in matrix rows and model ids.\n\nModel ids: \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\" etc. For DN350-600: \"DN350 class 1 or 2 Q3=2500\". Consistent format.\n\nNow dates: \"dated 16 September 2009\" → \"2009-09-16\". \n- 2009-09-16\n- 2010-04-09\n- 2011-05-02\n- 2012-03-28\n- 2013-07-18\n- 2015-03-18\n- 2016-03-30\n- 2020-12-30\n\nPages: 41, 40, 40, 40, 2, 6, 21, 27. Also annexes: schema has no annex field; could add \"annexes\"? Not in schema. I'll omit annexes or include as extra field? Omit to stay schema-clean. Hmm, annexes info is real; maybe include in test_reports as \"annexes\": 14? Schema lists id, date, pages, role. I'll keep to schema; the annex count lost. Eh — I could put pages: 41 and note annexes... I'll omit.\n\nNow certificate.date_issued: \"2023-06-12\".\n\nLet me also double-check page_total: \"Page 1 of 5\" → 5.\n\nNow assemble JSON. Sizes: matrix rows 36 with 7 columns; model_level 6 attributes × 36 values. Fine.\n\nActually — reconsider whether to include accuracy_class in model_level: values 2/1/\"1 or 2\". Yes include.\n\nRatio Q3/Q1 values: 400, 250, 160, 100.\n\nLet me now write the JSON carefully.\n\ncertificate:\n```json\n{\n  \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-NL1-20.02\",\n  \"revision\": \"2\",\n  \"scheme\": \"A\",\n  \"project_number\": \"3646819\",\n  \"page_total\": 5,\n  \"member_state\": \"The Netherlands\",\n  \"date_issued\": \"2023-06-12\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n}\n```\n\nissuing_authority:\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"NMi Certin B.V.\",\n  \"address_lines\": [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"],\n  \"person_responsible\": \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\",\n  \"person_title\": \"Certification Board\",\n  \"phone\": \"+31 88 636 2332\",\n  \"email\": \"certin@nmi.nl\",\n  \"website\": \"www.nmi.nl\",\n  \"oiml_issuer_id\": \"NL1\"\n}\n```\n\napplicants/manufacturers: one entry each, same data.\n\ncertified_type:\n```json\n{\n  \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n  \"type_designations\": [\"WATERFLUX 3070\"],\n  \"description\": \"An electromagnetic water meter\",\n  \"module_designation\": null\n}\n```\nmodule_designation null — schema allows null. Or omit. I'll omit to avoid noise? Schema includes it with example \"Not applicable\". The certified type is a complete meter; \"Not applicable\" reasonable. I'll set null... actually rule 8: don't fabricate; null is allowed by schema. I'll just omit it. Hmm, schema lists it with \"| null\" so null is fine. I'll omit — cleaner.\n\nmodel_family:\nfamily_name: null, models: 36 entries {model_id: \"...\"}.\n\nmodel_id format: \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\" — contains space and =. Fine.\n\nActually let me reconsider: maybe include label with flows for readability: label: \"DN25, accuracy class 2, Q1 0.025 m³/h, Q3 10 m³/h\". That duplicates matrix. I'll skip label (omit).\n\nNow build model_level:\n\nq1 values (36):\nDN25 c2 Q3=10: 0.025\nDN25 c2 Q3=16: 0.04\nDN40 c2 Q3=25: 0.0625\nDN40 c2 Q3=40: 0.1\nDN50 c2 Q3=40: 0.1\nDN50 c2 Q3=63: 0.1575\nDN65 c2 Q3=63: 0.1575\nDN65 c2 Q3=100: 0.25\nDN65 c1 Q3=100: 0.4\nDN80 c2 Q3=100: 0.25\nDN80 c2 Q3=160: 0.4\nDN80 c1 Q3=100: 0.625\nDN80 c1 Q3=160: 0.64\nDN100 c2 Q3=160: 0.4\nDN100 c2 Q3=250: 0.625\nDN100 c1 Q3=160: 1\nDN100 c1 Q3=250: 1\nDN125 c2 Q3=250: 0.625\nDN125 c2 Q3=400: 1\nDN125 c1 Q3=250: 1.5625\nDN125 c1 Q3=400: 1.6\nDN150 c2 Q3=400: 1\nDN150 c2 Q3=630: 1.575\nDN150 c1 Q3=400: 2.5\nDN150 c1 Q3=630: 2.52\nDN200 c2 Q3=630: 1.575\nDN200 c1 Q3=630: 3.9375\nDN250 c2 Q3=1000: 2.5\nDN250 c1 Q3=1000: 6.25\nDN300 c2 Q3=1600: 4\nDN300 c1 Q3=1600: 10\nDN350: 15.625\nDN400: 25\nDN450: 25\nDN500: 39.375\nDN600: 63\n\nq2:\n0.04, 0.064, 0.1, 0.16, 0.16, 0.252, 0.252, 0.4, 0.64, 0.4, 0.64, 1, 1.024, 0.64, 1, 1.6, 1.6, 1, 1.6, 2.5, 2.56, 1.6, 2.52, 4, 4.032, 2.52, 6.3, 4, 10, 6.4, 16, 25, 40, 40, 63, 100.8\n\nq3:\n10, 16, 25, 40, 40, 63, 63, 100, 100, 100, 160, 100, 160, 160, 250, 160, 250, 250, 400, 250, 400, 400, 630, 400, 630, 630, 630, 1000, 1000, 1600, 1600, 2500, 4000, 4000, 6300, 6300\n\nq4:\n12.5, 20, 31.25, 50, 50, 78.75, 78.75, 125, 125, 125, 200, 125, 200, 200, 312.5, 200, 312.5, 312.5, 500, 312.5, 500, 500, 787.5, 500, 787.5, 787.5, 787.5, 1250, 1250, 2000, 2000, 3125, 5000, 5000, 7875, 7875\n\nratio:\n400, 400, 400, 400, 400, 400, 400, 400, 250, 400, 400, 160, 250, 400, 400, 160, 250, 400, 400, 160, 250, 400, 400, 160, 250, 400, 160, 400, 160, 400, 160, 160, 160, 160, 160, 100\n\nLet me verify against table:\n- DN25 r1: 400 ✓; r2: 400 ✓\n- DN40: 400, 400 ✓\n- DN50: 400, 400 ✓\n- DN65: 400, 400, 250 ✓\n- DN80: 400, 400, 160, 250 ✓\n- DN100: 400, 400, 160, 250 ✓\n- DN125: 400, 400, 160, 250 ✓\n- DN150: 400, 400, 160, 250 ✓\n- DN200: 400, 160 ✓\n- DN250: 400, 160 ✓\n- DN300: 400, 160 ✓\n- DN350–500: 160 ✓; DN600: 100 ✓\n\naccuracy_class:\n2,2,2,2,2,2,2,2,1,2,2,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,1,2,1,2,1,\"1 or 2\",\"1 or 2\",\"1 or 2\",\"1 or 2\",\"1 or 2\"\n\nMatrix rows (36) with keys: meter_size, accuracy_class, q1, q2, q3, q4, ratio_q3_q1. Values as numbers.\n\nNow the config_level environmental_class. Let me finalize:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"environmental_class\",\n  \"axis\": \"software_version_and_installation\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": null, \"value\": \"M2\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"installed outdoors, software version 5.0.1_ or higher\", \"value\": \"O\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"installed in a building, software version 4.3.1_ or lower\", \"value\": \"B\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nHmm, condition null entry — the first cell \"M2\" standing alone. Acceptable.\n\nWait, actually re-reading: \"M2<br>O (installed outdoors) for software version 5.0.1_ or higher<br>B (installed in a building) for software version 4.3.1_ or lower\". Perhaps it reads \"M2 or O (installed outdoors) for sw 5.0.1_ or higher\" — i.e., class M2 or O? No... \"M2\" then newline \"O (installed outdoors) for...\". I think: environmental class M2; environmental class O for outdoor install (sw≥5.0.1); class B for building install (sw≤4.3.1). My encoding preserves it.\n\nambient_temperature_range config values include unit_symbol \"°C\" — schema config values {condition, value}; I'll add unit_symbol and unit_id: null. Fine.\n\nPower supply config:\n\npower_supply_voltage:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"power_supply_voltage\",\n  \"axis\": \"power_supply_option\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"replaceable 3.6 V D-cell lithium battery\", \"value\": 3.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"non-replaceable D-cell lithium battery (Flexpower, software version 5.0.1_ or higher)\", \"value\": 3.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"DC power supply (Flexpower, software version 5.0.1_ or higher)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 9, \"max\": 30}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"AC power supply (Flexpower, software version 5.0.1_ or higher)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 100, \"max\": 230}, \"unit_symbol\": \"V\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\npower_supply_frequency:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"power_supply_frequency\",\n  \"axis\": \"power_supply_option\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"AC power supply (Flexpower, software version 5.0.1_ or higher, tolerance ±2 %)\", \"value\": {\"min\": 50, \"max\": 60}, \"unit_symbol\": \"Hz\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\npower_supply_type:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"power_supply_type\",\n  \"axis\": \"power_supply_option\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"battery option\", \"value\": \"DC battery\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Flexpower DC option (software version 5.0.1_ or higher)\", \"value\": \"DC\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Flexpower AC option (software version 5.0.1_ or higher)\", \"value\": \"AC\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\nHmm \"DC battery\" — battery is DC. Keep simple: \"battery\", \"DC\", \"AC\".\n\nfield_current:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"field_current\",\n  \"axis\": \"software_version\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"software version 4.3.1_ or lower (only setting)\", \"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"mA\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"software version 5.0.1_ or higher (multiple selectable; shall be set to 16 mA)\", \"value\": 16, \"unit_symbol\": \"mA\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\ntype_level list finalize:\n```json\n{\n  \"measuring_principle\": {\"value\": \"Electromagnetic\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"electromagnetic_environment\": {\"value\": \"E2\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"water_temperature_class\": {\"value\": \"T50\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"water_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 0.1, \"max\": 50}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"maximum_admissible_pressure\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"MPa\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"orientation\": {\"value\": \"All positions\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"flow_profile_sensitivity_class\": {\"value\": \"U0 and D0\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"straight_pipe_length_upstream\": {\"value\": 0, \"unit_symbol\": \"DN\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"straight_pipe_length_downstream\": {\"value\": 0, \"unit_symbol\": \"DN\", \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"reverse_flow\": {\"value\": \"sensor designed to measure reverse flow\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"pressure_loss_class\": {\"value\": \"Δp63\", \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n  \"maximum_pressure_loss\": {\"value\": 0.63, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\", \"unit_id\": null}\n}\n```\n\nHmm \"orientation\": \"All positions (Horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\" — value string \"All positions\" plus detail? I'll use full \"All positions (horizontal, vertical or diagonal)\". Keep as-is minus formatting.\n\n\"Δp63\" — original \"Δp 63\". I'll write \"Δp63\" canonical. Value strings shouldn't include LaTeX; Δp is fine Unicode. OK.\n\nThe structured-values rule says every characteristic value MUST be object with value/unit_symbol/unit_id/footnote_markers. For type_level, that shape applies. Include footnote_markers: []? The example in rules shows footnote_markers present when referenced. I'll include footnote_markers: [] where none — or omit. The schema shape shows {value, unit, footnote_markers[]}. I'll include \"footnote_markers\": [] in each for consistency. Slight verbosity, fine.\n\nActually to keep it a bit lighter I'll include footnote_markers only where relevant... The rule says every characteristic value MUST be an object with that shape. I'll include footnote_markers: [] everywhere in type_level and model_level values. That adds bulk to 36×5 arrays... 180 empty arrays. Hmm. Acceptable? It's a lot of noise. The example in rule 4 for R137 model_level values shows {\"model\": \"G1.6\", \"value\": 2.5} WITHOUT footnote_markers/unit. So model_level values can be minimal ({model, value}). Good — keep model_level minimal. For type_level, follow strict shape with unit_symbol/unit_id; footnote_markers I'll include empty arrays... The rule 2 example: {\"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": \"u:degree_Celsius\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"1)\"]}. And rule 7 example shows {\"value\": {...}, \"unit_symbol\": \"g\", \"footnote_markers\": [\"1)\"]} — footnote_markers present when referenced. I'll include footnote_markers: [] on type_level entries. OK.\n\nunit_id: leave null per rule 3. Could set \"u:degree_Celsius\" for °C since rule 2 example shows it, but rule 3 says leave null unless certain. I'm fairly certain