01\".\n\nRole: I'll use \"type_evaluation_report\" for the first four (series R49-1/2006-NL1-xx) and for the NMi- ones... The text: \"The conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". The certificate earlier references \"OIML Type Evaluation Report\" (singular). I'll mark all eight as \"type_evaluation_report\". Acceptable.\n\nDocumentation folder T10201-11 → include as test_reports entry role \"documentation_file\", id \"T10201-11\". No date/pages — omit those keys.\n\nNow output size: the model_level with 6 attributes × 36 values = 216 lines. That's big but let's do it. Actually, should I include ratio as model_level? Yes. And accuracy_class.\n\nHmm, wait — is there a risk that including both matrix_tables rows and model_level is redundant overkill? The R137 example explicitly shows both. Follow it.\n\nLet me now also decide model_id strings. Use format \"DN25-class2-Q3=10\"? Or with spaces \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\". I'll use \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\" style; for class \"1 or 2\": \"DN350 class 1 or 2 Q3=2500\". Slightly awkward but fine.\n\nAlternatively model_id could simply be like \"DN25/Q3=10 (class 2)\". Keep my format.\n\nLet me write out all 36 model ids:\n1. \"DN25 class 2 Q3=10\"\n2. \"DN25 class 2 Q3=16\"\n3. \"DN40 class 2 Q3=25\"\n4. \"DN40 class 2 Q3=40\"\n5. \"DN50 class 2 Q3=40\"\n6. \"DN50 class 2 Q3=63\"\n7. \"DN65 class 2 Q3=63\"\n8. \"DN65 class 2 Q3=100\"\n9. \"DN65 class 1 Q3=100\"\n10. \"DN80 class 2 Q3=100\"\n11. \"DN80 class 2 Q3=160\"\n12. \"DN80 class 1 Q3=100\"\n13. \"DN80 class 1 Q3=160\"\n14. \"DN100 class 2 Q3=160\"\n15. \"DN100 class 2 Q3=250\"\n16. \"DN100 class 1 Q3=160\"\n17. \"DN100 class 1 Q3=250\"\n18. \"DN125 class 2 Q3=250\"\n19. \"DN125 class 2 Q3=400\"\n20. \"DN125 class 1 Q3=250\"\n21. \"DN125 class 1 Q3=400\"\n22. \"DN150 class 2 Q3=400\"\n23. \"DN150 class 2 Q3=630\"\n24. \"DN150 class 1 Q3=400\"\n25. \"DN150 class 1 Q3=630\"\n26. \"DN200 class 2 Q3=630\"\n27. \"DN200